    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. can you go into a bit more detail on why the solubility drops off as it gets more alkaline? like, what's happening at the chemical level with the ions? also, if the client sees signs of deficiency, what are the typical visual symptoms in the leaves?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "As soil $\\text{pH}$ increases (becomes more alkaline), metal ions like $\\text{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\\text{Mn}^{2+}$ react with hydroxide ions ($\\text{OH}^-$) in the soil water. This reaction causes these soluble ions to precipitate, forming insoluble hydroxides or oxides that are unavailable for plant uptake. Symptomatically, iron deficiency typically causes chlorosis (yellowing) between the veins on new, young leaves. Manganese deficiency often presents as interveinal chlorosis, which can appear on older leaves sooner than iron deficiency.",
      "source": "target",
